From 6efe7c01dc37ccb468997e78d3ef3f9a5cd8db98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:49:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] dnsmasq: Wallguard's lookup gate and one shared threat table
 (--wallguard-dir, --reaper-nx-db)

--wallguard-dir points every instance at Wallguard's compiled table (sorted
64-bit name hashes + list masks, subtree bit, allow table), its policy file
and its run/ folder. A query is judged after the router's own answers
(address=, local=, Dreadwatch), on the UDP and TCP paths, and every name of an
answer's CNAME chain is checked (CNAME cloaking; such answers are not cached).
Verdicts: 0.0.0.0/:: or NXDOMAIN, safe-search answers, pauses, per-profile
lists and blocked services, by MAC or network. A record names the list entry
that matched, never the name as asked. --reaper-nx-db maps Dreadwatch's threat
names from the same table format, read-only and shared by every instance
(12 bytes a name once instead of ~54 in each). Both tables are validated
(magic, version, counts, exact size) before use and re-stat'ed every 2 s; a
missing or damaged table blocks nothing. rednsd's client-address option is
believed only from loopback (edns0.c), so Encrypted DNS devices are judged by
their real address.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
